n: 3.6.4.5
name: c-for-bootstrap
kind: finding
is: The reading concludes that C is the smallest and most portable language for a bootstrap of about 1,500 lines that calls a few libc functions and is then replaced by code the language emits, and that the old runtime's fragility came from growing the C, not from C. A private note records that the retired Go kernel kept each compiled primitive twice.
status: inferred
from: 3.6.3.1 3.6.2.1 3.6.4.3 3.6.1.4 3.6.3.7
conf: mid
limit: The reading's points against Go (per-thread write toggle, movable stacks, a runtime that owns signals and collection) and about Rust came from general knowledge and were not checked in the reading.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.6.8
name: keep
kind: hub
is: Six things the reading would carry forward from the old runtime: a one-command build with no dependency; a compiled function kept as an ordinary node with its original body as fallback; one page allocation for all machine code; a numbered helper table with the emitter written in the language; call counts and a sampler as compile triggers; identity from content with a sealed binary store. The reading's list also names in-process cells, interfaces that answer a value or nothing with a reason word, handles of index plus generation, and a fault handler that writes one row.
at: 2026-11-24

n: 3.6.8.2
name: compiled-function-is-a-node
kind: finding
is: Keep the dispatch form in which a compiled function is an ordinary node the evaluator already reads, holding the machine code's index and the original body. Dispatch costs nothing extra, and a call the compiled code does not cover falls back to the original body instead of failing.
status: inferred
